WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.407
Suppose your application needs to find records whose status is delayed. For each

00:00:05.407 --> 00:00:11.220
record, ask one question: is its status equal to delayed? The answer is either true or

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:16.965
false. A condition like this is called a predicate. That word names something simple:

00:00:16.965 --> 00:00:22.778
a test that a record either passes or fails. We will build the rest of the system from

00:00:22.778 --> 00:00:23.725
this one idea.

00:00:28.225 --> 00:00:34.185
Here is the same question written as JSON. Field tells us which part of the record to

00:00:34.185 --> 00:00:39.725
inspect. Operator tells us how to compare it. Value is what we compare it with.

00:00:39.725 --> 00:00:45.685
Together they say: status equals delayed. JSON is a way to represent structured data.

00:00:45.685 --> 00:00:51.716
Here, it represents the question itself. A person, a visual editor, or an AI agent can

00:00:51.716 --> 00:00:53.750
all produce this same object.

00:00:58.250 --> 00:01:03.789
Now ask for records that are both delayed and Express priority. The all group means

00:01:03.789 --> 00:01:08.927
every condition inside it must pass. Change the group to any, and one passing

00:01:08.927 --> 00:01:14.600
condition is enough. These groups can contain other groups. That is nesting: a larger

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:20.273
question built from smaller questions. You can read the rule from the outside inward,

00:01:20.273 --> 00:01:21.875
one condition at a time.

00:01:26.375 --> 00:01:31.266
The rules engine is the software that interprets these conditions and evaluates them

00:01:31.266 --> 00:01:36.215
against data. Given the same predicate and the same inputs, it applies the same tests

00:01:36.215 --> 00:01:41.165
consistently. The AI can reason about which question to ask; the engine decides which

00:01:41.165 --> 00:01:46.172
records satisfy the resulting rule. In this demo, predicates select records or produce

00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:51.122
permission decisions. They do not themselves send emails or perform business actions.

00:01:51.122 --> 00:01:54.150
Your application chooses what to do with the result.

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:03.995
Our engine supports two useful paths. Check evaluates a predicate against data already

00:02:03.995 --> 00:02:09.279
supplied to it. To Prisma translates supported predicates into a database query plan,

00:02:09.279 --> 00:02:14.563
so the database can find matching records. Here, both paths answer the same question.

00:02:14.563 --> 00:02:19.908
More advanced rules can describe related records, counts, groups, and ordered windows.

00:02:19.908 --> 00:02:25.192
Some need several query steps; some are evaluated with Check. The engine reports what

00:02:25.192 --> 00:02:26.125
it can support.

00:02:30.625 --> 00:02:36.196
Instead of giving an agent an unrestricted database connection, your application can

00:02:36.196 --> 00:02:41.700
ask it to propose a predicate. The agent first receives a description of what it is

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:46.940
allowed to query. It produces JSON, the engine validates that JSON, and only an

00:02:46.940 --> 00:02:52.644
allowed query is executed. This separates deciding what to ask from enforcing what may

00:02:52.644 --> 00:02:57.950
run. The next lesson explains the description of that permitted surface: a lens.

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:07.707
These rules are serializable: you can turn them into JSON, store them, load them, and

00:03:07.707 --> 00:03:12.469
edit them while your application is running. Changing a rule does not require

00:03:12.469 --> 00:03:17.726
deploying new application code. Validation still happens before use. We use shipments

00:03:17.726 --> 00:03:22.859
here because their fields and relationships make useful examples. The same building

00:03:22.859 --> 00:03:27.993
blocks can describe your own application data. This is the engine for your AI SaaS;

00:03:27.993 --> 00:03:30.899
the shipping interface is only a demonstration.
